  "finalAnswer": "For general medical education, anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening allergic reaction. Common symptoms that involve the breathing and circulation systems include:\n\n**Breathing Difficulties:**\n*   Swelling of the throat, tongue, or lips\n*   Difficulty breathing or feeling short of breath\n*   Wheezing, which is a high-pitched whistling sound when breathing out\n*   Stridor, which may indicate an airway obstruction\n*   A feeling of tightness in the throat\n\n**Circulation Concerns:**\n*   Low blood pressure (hypotension)\n*   Weak pulse\n*   Rapid heartbeat (tachycardia)\n*   Dizziness, lightheadedness, or fainting\n*   Loss of consciousness\n\nThese signs can appear rapidly and affect multiple body systems simultaneously. Because you mentioned you are not experiencing these symptoms, you likely do not have an active anaphylactic reaction, but this information is for educational purposes only and does not replace professional medical advice.",
